const s=9,e="Heavy Metal and DO Affect Survival and Reproduction of Mollusc",a="Gulab Bind and Asha Rani",t=[118,125],i=["Department of Zoology","Tilak Dhari P.G. College, Jaunpur, Affiliated to V.B.S. Purvanchal University,","Jaunpur, Uttar Pradesh","*Corresponding Author-","Dr. Asha Rani","Department of Zoology","Tilak Dhari P.G. College, Jaunpur, Affiliated to V.B.S. Purvanchal University,","Jaunpur, Uttar Pradesh, India, 222002","Email-anshuaucky@gmail.com"],o=[{t:"h",x:"ABSTRACT:"},{t:"p",x:"Heavy metal and DO (Dissolved Oxygen) pollution is a worldwide issue with serious consequences. Heavy metals are continuously discharged into the aquatic ecosystem and persistently accumulate in water as well as sediments. This affects the health of the aquatic organisms and the accumulated heavy metals eventually poses a direct threat to human health as they move up the food chain. In molluscs like L. marginalis, hypoxia serves as a major stressor that suppresses growth and reduces the development of reproductive systems. To maintain oxygen homeostasis under hypoxic conditions, these molluscs increase their ventilation and circulation rates. This physiological response forces more water over the gills, which unfortunately facilitates the rapid uptake and accumulation of toxic elements such as mercury and cadmium. Focused studies on the freshwater snail Lymnaea stagnalis reveal the devastating impact of cadmium on the reproductive process. At concentrations of 400 μg Cd2+liter−1, egg production is completely terminated, and at 200 μg/L (at 20°C), the hatching rate drops to just 0.4%. The most critical vulnerability occurs during the embryonic stage; high levels of cadmium can lock eggs in the initial cleavage phase, while 100 and 200 μg Cd2+liter−1, egg growth was halted; development at the cleavage, gastrula and pre-hatching and also affects the egg masses. Even at concentrations of 25 to 100 μg Cd2+liter−1, the developmental timeline is stretched, with hatching occurring up to 15 days later than in uncontaminated controls. Furthermore, a very low amount of cadmium can also affect Calcium channels and create hypocalcemia conditions That leads to muscle spasms, then they die. Metals like mercury (Hg) and lead (Pb) can cause histological changes in gonads, leading to reduced fertilization success."},{t:"p",x:"Keywords: Heavy metal, DO (Dissolved Oxygen), Hypoxia, L. marginalis, Lymnaea stagnalis, Cadmium, Hypocalcemia"},{t:"h",x:"INTRODUCTION:"},{t:"p",x:"World reveal that it is heavily polluted, various chemicals from anthropogenic activities continuing to increase fast growing industrialization and urbanization in many regions. The marine ecosystem is polluted by agricultural fertilizers, sewerage, industrial effluents. Which have an impact on marine life. Heavy metals in river come mainly from naturally occurring geochemical materials. However, ecosystems are contaminated by heavy metals and organic chemicals that can be absorbed and accumulated by crop plants. While the ground water is extremely affected by industrial effluent."},{t:"h",x:"CHAPTER 9"},{t:"h",x:"HEAVY METAL AND DO AFFECT SURVIVAL AND REPRODUCTION OF MOLLUSC"},{t:"p",x:"Freshwater molluscs, including bivalves and gastropods, are highly sensitive to changes in aquatic environments because of their sedentary behaviour, filter-feeding habit, and continuous exposure to pollutants present in water and sediments (Naimo, 1995). Freshwater molluscs are mostly sedentary in nature and do not move frequently. As a result, they are highly affected by water contamination. Due to these characteristics, molluscs are also used as bioindicators for aquatic pollution."},{t:"p",x:"According to (Pund and Kurhe, 2023) molluscs occupy a variety of aquatic and land-based niches. Being filter feeders (Bivalves) they bioaccumulate a number of contaminants in their tissues at a higher rate. These metals induces oxidative stress, DNA damage and changes in chromatin structure, which may affect negatively impact on fertility."},{t:"p",x:"Dissolved oxygen is also a very important environmental factor that affects the life of freshwater molluscs. It is essential for respiration, energy production, and many other physiological processes. The concentration of dissolved oxygen in freshwater ecosystems is influenced by temperature, eutrophication, microbial decomposition, organic pollution, and water flow dynamics (Sime, 2005). Reduction in dissolved oxygen levels, commonly referred to as hypoxia, imposes severe physiological stress on molluscs and other aquatic organisms. Under hypoxic conditions, molluscs reduce their metabolic activities by decreasing feeding rate, shell opening, and locomotion in order to conserve energy (Sime, 2005)."},{t:"p",x:'As fluctuations in oxygen levels can affect the growth, survival and gonadal development of freshwater pearl mussel Lamellidens marginalis (Kafi et al., 2021). Rising CO2 levels contribute to elevated water temperatures, then reduce the solubility of dissolved oxygen levels (Keeling et al. 2010, Doney et al. 2012). Filter feeding bivalves are an important component of aquatic ecosystems, they are generally considered ecosystem engineers" by mitigating the effects of eutrophication (Officer et al., 1982). Juvenile freshwater mussels are sensitive to low dissolved oxygen (Hyvarinen et al., 2022).'},{t:"h",x:"2. MOLLUSCS ARE BIOINDICATORS:"},{t:"p",x:"The use of freshwater gastropods and bivalves has become a cornerstone of bioindication and environmental monitoring programs. These organisms provide critical data regarding the ecological health of freshwater systems and help in measuring the concentration of various pollutants. As noted by Markert et al. (1999) and Zuykov et al. (2013), their suitability for such research is due to several physiological and ecological traits: their significant body size, restricted mobility, high population density, and the relative ease with which they can be collected and categorized."},{t:"p",x:'The historical recognition of biological indicators draws a parallel to 19th-century coal mining, where the presence of "bad air" or methane was a known hazard; similarly, it has long been established that molluscs act as sentinels for aquatic health. Over a century ago, Ortmann (1909) in North America and Kolkwitz and Marsson (1909) in Germany documented that the disappearance or reduction of freshwater mussel communities serves as a clear indicator of environmental contamination.'},{t:"p",x:"Furthermore, Sharma and Sharma (2015) highlight that molluscs possess a unique combination of sensitivity and resilience toward habitat changes and chemical stressors. Specifically, benthic species that reside within the substrate often act as deposit feeders. Because these organisms directly consume organic matter from the riverbed, they are prone to higher levels of heavy metal bioaccumulation compared to other aquatic groups (Romadhona et al., 2018)."},{t:"h",x:"3. EFFECT OF HEAVY METALS ON MOLLUSC:"},{t:"p",x:"Heavy metal pollution is a direct threat to the physiological and cellular life of freshwater molluscs. In a 2025 study, Guria and colleagues took a close look at Bellamya bengalensis, an edible snail common in our local waters. They found that metals like Arsenic, Lead, and Mercury aren't just pollutants—they are potent stressors. These toxins get inside the snail and mess up essential chemical reactions. Whether it's a snail (gastropod) or a clam (bivalve), the result is often the same: they stop moving, stop breeding, and eventually, the population dies out. This is a major driver of ecological decay in rivers today."},{t:"h",x:"3.1 Bioaccumulation and Biomagnifications:"},{t:"p",x:"Molluscs accumulate heavy metals via direct uptake from the water through gills and mantle or eating contaminated algae or sediment detritus. Metals like Lead (Pb) and Cadmium (Cd) have a high affinity for sulfur-containing ligands. They bind to specific proteins called metallothioneins, which act as a storage system within the snail tissues Kagi and Schaffer (1988). Metals accumulation is not distributed evenly because different organs have different metabolic roles. In gastropods the highest concentrations are mostly found in the digestive gland and the kidneys Amiard et al. (2006). Concentration beat a certain threshold, it causes oxidative stress and cellular damage, then increase mortality rates. (Adesakin et al., 2026). When a predator eats several contaminated molluscs, they ingest the entire accumulated dose of those individuals, High concentrations of metals passed up in the food chain. ultimately impacting human health through seafood consumption (Sawangproh and Paejaroen, 2025)."},{t:"h",x:"3.2 Disruption of Calcium Metabolism:"},{t:"p",x:'The process of Ionic Mimicry, many heavy metal ions have a similar size and charge to calcium ions, Cadmium and Lead mimic calcium, they use the same calcium channels in to cells. They act as a competitive inhibitor, and metals inhibit calcium ATPase (Tchounwou et al., 2012). Calcium acts as a "second messenger" in cells. The disruption of metabolism, shells become thin and fragile and slow growth due to the metabolic cost of detoxification (Banaee, M. 2024). Under the influence of heavy metals, molluscs lose the capacity to keep their shells tightly closed. Consequently, they become easy prey for predators, and their internal tissues are exposed to a greater concentration of pollutants present in the water (Gheorghe et al., 2017; Ochoa-Esteso et al., 2024).'},{t:"h",x:"3.3 Cellular and Genetic Damage:"},{t:"p",x:"At the cellular level, heavy metal induces DNA damage and production of the oxidative stress. Free metal ions set off a chain reaction that attacks lipid membranes, leading to high permeability .Metals accumulate in the mitochondria and disrupt the electron transport chain .In the lysosome , the accumulation of heavy metals in excessive amounts organelles leads to lysosomal destabilization therefore digestive enzymes leak into the cytoplasm causing cellular autolysis Ochoa-Esteso et al. (2024) .High levels of Lead and Mercury break DNA strands and leading to mutation or apoptosis, and also inhibit essential repair enzymes (Gheorghe et al.2017)."},{t:"h",x:"3.4 The Critical Role of DO and Metal Uptake:"},{t:"p",x:"Dissolved Oxygen (DO) is a fundamental limiting factor for the survival of freshwater molluscs. Under hypoxic conditions, mollusc begin to exhibit hyperventilation to capture more oxygen Gheorghe et al. (2017). Consequently, heavy metals pass through the gills and accumulate in greater quantities in their tissues. Some gastropods move towards the water surface to reach the oxygenated layer. They become easy prey for predators. Metallothioneins are proteins that helps neutralize or excretion of metals. The biosynthesis of Metallothioneins requires high amount of ATP amiard et al. (2006). Under low oxygen condition cells lack sufficient ATP, cease the production of their protective proteins. Metals reached rapidly inside the cell Sokolova et al. (2004)"},{t:"h",x:"4. Survival and Reproduction:"},{t:"p",x:"The survival and reproductive success of freshwater molluscs is governed by the interaction between toxic chemicals and oxygen availability (Gheorghe et al., 2017). while they can survive individual stressors. The combined effect of low oxygen and heavy metal toxicity causes severe damage, leading to rapid collapse of the entire population."},{t:"h",x:"4.1 Vulnerability of Early life stage:"},{t:"p",x:"Chronic sulfate exposure significantly reduced mussel growth, foot movement, and relative water content in juvenile mussels of M. margaritifera. Mussels at younger stages were not necessarily more sensitive to sulphate, Hyvarinen et al. (2024). According to Kumar et al. (2024), In the Juvenile stage of molluscs Pb and Cd reduce juvenile survival up to 70% as compared to adults, in hatching periods, when eggs were exposed to heavy metals then the number of offspring declined. As noted by Hyvärinen et al. (2024) in M. margaritifera, this results in the retardation of shell growth and reduced foot movement. A smaller juvenile, drift away then the chance of becoming prey increases."},{t:"h",x:"4.2 Effect on Reproduction and Development:"},{t:"p",x:"When a water body is unpolluted, and fully oxygenated, molluscs utilize their energy in egg production and reproduction. Heavy metals are abundant and dissolved in the water, metals results in growth retardations. Specifically, Lead (Pb) and Cadmium (Cd) more effective endocrine disruptors. In mollusc hormones are interfered by the heavy metal (Gagné et al., 2001). Inconsistency in oxygen levels significantly impact the growth, survival, and gonadal development of the freshwater pearl mussel, Lamellidens marginalis (Kafi et al., 2021). The synergistic effect of hypoxia and heavy metals interferes with the neurohormonal changes that leads to postponement of eggs laying until polluted water conditions improve (Sokolova et al., 2012)."},{t:"h",x:"4.3 Impact on Life Cycle and Spawning Success:"},{t:"p",x:"Heavy metals and critical transition phase in the mollusc life. Some molluscs depend on external signals to spawn simultaneously. Pollutants can slow their sensory receptors, resulting in asynchronous spawning, therefore eggs and sperm are released at different times rather than simultaneously. which significantly decreases the fertilization rate also affects the survival and gonadal development of fresh water pearl mussel, Lamellidens marginalis (Geraerts & Joosse, 1984; Oehlmann & Schulte-Oehlmann, 2003; Kafi et al., 2021)"},{t:"h",x:"5. PHYSIOLOGICAL AND BEHAVIORAL RESPONSE:"},{t:"p",x:"Molluscs are very sensitive to their habitat. Little disturbance in their habitat (like heavy metal pollution) leads to drastic change in their feeding physiology and behavior. Their feeding behavior is also act as a critical bioindicator. However, molluscs have a tendency to accumulate some number of heavy metals in their soft tissue and shell (Di Donato G, Davolos D, Chimenti C, De Matthaeis E. 2020)."},{t:"h",x:"5.1 Physiological response"},{t:"p",x:"Physiological inhibition occurs due to internal biological changes caused by heavy metals and dissolved oxygen level."},{t:"p",x:"Enzymatic Inhibition and Detoxification Stress: Heavy metals like Lead (Pb) and Cadmium (Cd) bind to the sulfhydryl groups of proteins, inhibiting key metabolic enzymes involved in carbohydrate and protein metabolism (Banaee, 2024). This biochemical change leads to decreased feeding due to damage occurring in the digestive system."},{t:"p",x:"Impact of low DO on metabolism: In environments with low DO (hypoxia), molluscs minimize energy demand through metabolic rate depression (Kotsyuba & Dyachuk, 2023). Under hypoxic conditions, molluscs shift from aerobic to anaerobic metabolism to maintain energy production in the absence of sufficient oxygen"},{t:"p",x:"(de Zwaan and Wijsman, 1976). Low dissolved oxygen greatly impacts the metabolism of molluscs. Due to oxygen deficiency, they enter a dormant state with reduced metabolic activity."},{t:"h",x:"Behavioural Responses:"},{t:"p",x:"Behavioural changes are the primary defence mechanisms of molluscs against the reduced intake of toxic substances. Some behavioural changes also occur due to low dissolved oxygen levels. 5.2.1 Shell Closure: A primary behavioural response to both low DO and heavy metal toxicity in bivalves is the closure of their shells (Kotsyuba & Dyachuk, 2023). This regulates the entry of heavy metals into the body. Molluscs stop external processes such as filter feeding and nearly disconnect themselves from the surrounding environment."},{t:"p",x:"5.2.2 Avoidance behaviour: Hypoxia-sensitive species may employ avoidance behaviours, moving to areas with higher oxygen content, whereas hypoxia-tolerant species (like oysters and mussels) typically rely on the aforementioned physiological adjustments (Kotsyuba & Dyachuk, 2023). Molluscs relocate from areas with low dissolved oxygen levels to areas with higher dissolved oxygen levels to improve their survival and lifespan."},{t:"h",x:"6. METHODOLOGIES FOR ASSESSING MOLLUSC DIVERSITY AND PHYSICO-CHEMICAL PARAMETERS"},{t:"l",x:["6.1 Mollusc Assessment Methodology:","Parameter Method/Instrument Description","Sampling Quadrat & Hand-netting","Quantitative collection of molluscs from the coastal and","bottom of the river."]},{t:"p",x:"Cleaning Soft Brushing Removal of periphyton and sediment from the shell surface without damaging the outermost organic layer of mollusc."},{t:"p",x:"Preservation 70% Ethanol Standard preservative for maintaining soft tissue integrity and shell morphology."},{t:"p",x:"Identification Conchological Keys Species-level identification based on shell height, coil twist count, and aperture shape."},{t:"h",x:"Survival"},{t:"p",x:"Analysis Tactile Stimulus Test Assessing mortality by observing the lack of foot retraction upon mechanical stimulation. Reproduction Fecundity Counting Monitoring the number of egg masses and individual eggs deposited per breeding cycle."},{t:"l",x:["Diversity Index Shannon-Wiener","Index","Mathematical analysis of species richness and distribution","within the mollusc community.","6.2 Physicochemical & Stressor Assessment:"]},{t:"h",x:"Parameter Method/Instrument Description"},{t:"p",x:"Temperature Digital Thermistor Measurement of water temperature, which regulates the metabolic rate of mollusc. pH Digital pH Electrode Monitoring acidity; low pH levels can lead to shell erosion and calcium loss."},{t:"p",x:"Dissolved Oxygen Optical DO Sensor Precise measurement of Hypoxia levels to study their synergistic effect with metals."},{t:"p",x:"Heavy Metals ICP-MS / AAS Detection of trace metals in water and sediment samples. Bioaccumulation Wet Acid Digestion Decomposition of soft tissues to measure internal metal loading. Water Hardness EDTA Titration Determines calcium carbonate levels, which significantly influence metal toxicity."},{t:"h",x:"7. CONCLUSION:"},{t:"p",x:'The survival and reproductive success of freshwater molluscs are tightly linked to the synergistic relationship between dissolved oxygen and heavy metal slevel concentrations. The research reveals that hypoxia actively accelerates the bioaccumulation of heavy metals like cadmium and mercury by forcing molluscs to increase their ventilation rates. This physiological demand creates a irreversible damage where the organism’s attempt to maintain oxygen homeostasis leads to a more rapid internal loading of toxins. At the cellular level, the occurrence of ionic mimicry allows heavy metals to cease calcium channels, leading to hypocalcemia, skeletal fragility, and the failure of essential "second messenger" systems. This disruption is most devastating during the early life stages, where even low concentrations of cadmium that cease the production of eggs at 400 μg/L can arrest embryonic development at the first cleavage, also leading to a significantly delay hatching. Moreover, the highly metabolic cost of synthesizing protective metallothionein proteins requires significant ATP, which is absent in hypoxic conditions, leaving the organism’s DNA and lipid membranes vulnerable against oxidative stress.'},{t:"p",x:"Ultimately, the decreases the number of species such as Lymnaea stagnalis and Lamellidens marginalis serves as a critical sentinel for ecosystem decay. Because these molluscs are filter feeders that bridge the gap between primary production and higher trophic levels, their physiological collapse through asynchronous spawning and increased juvenile mortality triggers a chain reaction that destabilizes the aquatic food web and create a direct risk to human health through the biomagnification of metals in the food chain. Effective sustainability management must therefore prioritize the maintenance of highly dissolved oxygen levels as a primary defense mechanism to mitigate the toxicological impact of heavy metal pollution."},{t:"h",x:"REFERENCES:"},{t:"p",x:"1. Adesakin, T. A., Adedeji, A. A., Aduwo, I. A., Oyewale, A. T., Adewole, H. A., & Omoshehin, T. O. (2026). Macroinvertebrate diversity, bioavailability, and bioaccumulation of heavy metals reveal ecological stress in freshwater bodies of Southwest Nigeria. Scientific African."},{t:"p",x:"2. Amiard, J. C., Amiard-Triquet, C., Barka, S., Pellerin, J., and Rainbow, P. S. (2006). Metallothioneins in aquatic invertebrates: Their role in toxicity and biomonitoring. Aquatic Toxicology, 76(2), 160–202. 3. APHA. (2017). Standard methods for the examination of water and wastewater (23rd ed.). American Public Health Association."},{t:"p",x:"4. Araujo, M., and Silva, M. A. (1998). The combined effects of temperature and metals copper, zinc and mercury on the embryological development of the mangrove oyster, Crassostrea rhizophorae. Restoration Ecology."},{t:"p",x:"5. Banaee, M. (2024). Perspective Chapter: Exploring the toxicity effect of heavy metals on aquatic organisms – A comprehensive analysis. IntechOpen. https://doi.org/10.5772/intechopen.1006890. 6. Brokordt, K., Defranchi, Y., Espósito, I., Cárcamo, C., Schmitt, P., Mercado, L., ... & Rivera-Ingraham, G. A. (2019). Reproduction immunity trade-off in a mollusk: hemocyte energy metabolism underlies cellular and molecular immune responses. Frontiers in Physiology, 10, 77."},{t:"p",x:"7. Di Donato, G., Davolos, D., Chimenti, C., & De Matthaeis, E. (2020). A genotoxicity study on Talitrus saltator (Crustacea, Amphipoda) exposed to lead and benzo(a)pyrene. The European Zoological Journal, 87(1), 235–241."},{t:"p",x:"8. Gagné, F., Blaise, C., and Hellou, J. (2001). Endocrine disruption and reproductive effects in freshwater mussels exposed to municipal effluents. Hydrobiologia, 446/447, 315–324."},{t:"p",x:"9. Gomot, A. (1998). Toxic effects of cadmium on reproduction, development, and hatching in the freshwater snaillymnaea stagnalisfor water quality monitoring. Ecotoxicology and environmental safety, 41(3), 288-297. 10. Gheorghe, S., et al. (2017). Metals toxic effects in aquatic ecosystems: Modulators of water quality. In Water Quality. IntechOpen."},{t:"p",x:"11. Hyvärinen, H. S., Sjönberg, T., Marjomäki, T. J., and Taskinen, J. (2022). Effect of low dissolved oxygen on the viability of juvenile Margaritifera margaritifera: Hypoxia tolerance ex situ. Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems, 32(8), 1393–1400."},{t:"p",x:"12. Hyvärinen, H., Keskinen, A., and Saarinen, M. (2024). Sulfate sensitivity of early life stages of freshwater mussels Unio crassus and Margaritifera margaritifera. Environmental Pollution."},{t:"p",x:"13. Kafi, M. A., Ahmed, M. S., and Fatema, K. (2021). Impact of heavy metal (lead) on the survival, gonadal development and fertilization of freshwater pearl mussel (Lamellidens marginalis). Journal of Fisheries, 9(1), 91307."},{t:"p",x:"14. Kagi, J. H., and Schaffer, A. (1988). Biochemistry of metallothionein. Biochemistry, 27(23), 8509–8515. 15. Kotsyuba, E., and Dyachuk, V. (2023). Role of the neuroendocrine system of marine bivalves in their response to hypoxia. International Journal of Molecular Sciences, 24(2), 1202."},{t:"p",x:"16. Lau, S., Mohamed, M., Yen, A. T. C., & Su'Ut, S. (1998). Accumulation of heavy metals in freshwater molluscs. Science of the total environment, 214(1-3), 113-121."},{t:"p",x:"17. Naimo, T.J. A review of the effects of heavy metals on freshwater mussels. Ecotoxicology 4, 341–362 (1995). https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00118870"},{t:"p",x:"18. Ochoa-Esteso, C., Roselló-Carrió, A., Carrasco-Correa, E. J., & Lerma-García, M. J. (2024). Bioaccumulation of environmental pollutants and marine toxins in bivalve molluscs: a review. Exploration of Foods and Foodomics, 2(6), 788-809."},{t:"p",x:"19. Oehlmann, J., and Schulte-Oehlmann, U. (2003). Endocrine disruption in molluscs. Pure and Applied Chemistry, 75(11-12), 2243–2261."},{t:"p",x:"20. Officer, C. B., Smayda, T. J., and Mann, R. (1982). Benthic filter feeding: A natural eutrophication control. Marine Ecology Progress Series, 9, 203–210."},{t:"p",x:"21. Rashid, P. (2021). Effects of dissolved oxygen concentrations on growth, survival and gonadal development of freshwater pearl mussel Lamellidens marginalis. Kafi MFH, Islam MH, Uddin MH, Sumon KA, Rashid H, 99-108."},{t:"p",x:"22. Ravera, O. (1991). Influence of heavy metals on the reproduction and embryonic development of freshwater pulmonates (Gastropoda; Mollusca) and cladocerans (Crustacea; Arthropoda). Comparative Biochemistry and Physiology Part C: Comparative Pharmacology, 100(1-2), 215-219. 23. Romadhona, G. V. S., Ridho, M. R., and Purwiyanto, P. (2018). Freshwater molluscs as bioindicator of Fe and Mn contamination in Lematang River, South Sumatera, Indonesia. Journal of Physics: Conference Series."},{t:"p",x:"24. Savage, N. B. (1975). Responses of selected bivalve molluscs to temperature and dissolved oxygen. Marine Biology."},{t:"p",x:"25. Sawangproh, W., and Paejaroen, S. (2025). Impact of heavy metals on freshwater gastropods and implications for human health. Journal of Environmental Science."},{t:"p",x:"26. Shannon, C. E., & Weaver, W. (1949). The mathematical theory of communication. University of Illinois Press."},{t:"p",x:"27. Sokolova, I. M., et al. (2012). Energy homeostasis and cellular stress responses in aquatic invertebrates. Marine Environmental Research, 79, 1–15."},{t:"p",x:"28. Subba Rao, N. V. (1989). Handbook: Freshwater molluscs of India. Zoological Survey of India. 24. Sime, I. (2005). A literature review of the water quality requirements of freshwater pearl mussels (Margaritifera margaritifera) and related freshwater bivalves. Scottish Natural Heritage Commissioned Report No. 084."},{t:"p",x:"25. Tchounwou, P. B., et al. (2012). Heavy metal toxicity and the environment. Molecular, Clinical and Environmental Toxicology, 101, 133–164."},{t:"p",x:"26. Trotta, M. C., et al. (2023). A metabolomic analysis to assess the responses of the male gonads of Mytilus galloprovincialis after heavy metal exposure. Marine Drugs, 21(12), 614."}],n={n:9,title:e,authors:a,pages:t,affiliations:i,blocks:o};export{i as affiliations,a as authors,o as blocks,n as default,s as n,t as pages,e as title};
